## Search with Option

Finding something in a collection is the perfect real-world use of Option. The result is binary: found or not found.

### Find a Name in a List

```xiom
fn find_name(name: Str) -> Option[Str] {
  if name == "Alice" { return Some("Found: Alice"); }
  if name == "Bob"   { return Some("Found: Bob"); }
  if name == "Carol" { return Some("Found: Carol"); }
  return None;
}

fn main() -> Int {
  let result1 = find_name("Bob");
  let result2 = find_name("Dave");

  io.println(result1.unwrap_or("Not found"));
  io.println(result2.unwrap_or("Not found"));
  return 0;
}
```

### Search with Extra Information

Instead of just returning a match message, return the actual data:

```xiom
fn get_age(name: Str) -> Option[Int] {
  if name == "Alice" { return Some(14); }
  if name == "Bob"   { return Some(16); }
  if name == "Carol" { return Some(15); }
  return None;
}

fn main() -> Int {
  if let Some(age) = get_age("Alice") {
    io.println("Alice is " + string.int_to_str(age));
  } else {
    io.println("Person not found");
  }
  return 0;
}
```

### Search with Validation

Combine multiple checks — find a value and then validate it:

```xiom
fn find_valid_score(name: Str) -> Option[Int] {
  let score = if name == "Alice" { Some(85) }
    else if name == "Bob" { Some(105) }  // invalid!
    else { None };

  if let Some(s) = score {
    if s >= 0 && s <= 100 {
      return Some(s);
    }
  }
  return None;
}

fn main() -> Int {
  io.println(find_valid_score("Alice").unwrap_or(-1));  // 85
  io.println(find_valid_score("Bob").unwrap_or(-1));    // -1 (score 105 was invalid)
  io.println(find_valid_score("Dave").unwrap_or(-1));   // -1 (not found)
  return 0;
}
```

